# MoveTo.action
#
# The ROS2-action form of problog_project's MoveTo BT node (see
# module/contracts/schema.yaml's own MoveTo entry). An action, not a
# service like PlanPath/EvaluateCondition, because a walk is durative --
# it runs for a real duration and must be cancellable mid-flight --
# exactly the "one startMoveto/haltMoveto pair run to its halt" contract
# schema.yaml itself describes.
#
# Server: amiga_ros2_planners' move_to_node, which samples `control_points`
# (typically PlanPath's own output, via the shared blackboard-key
# convention a future BT.cpp MoveTo leaf would carry over from PlanWith)
# into a nav_msgs/Path and drives it through Nav2's own controller_server
# FollowPath action -- see move_to_node.py's own module docstring for the
# full translation. Only takes the input needed to execute the walk: no
# triggers port -- a battery-depleted or collision-detected halt is an
# always-on safety cutoff (safety_monitor.py), not something a caller
# configures. `status` below is still the one true/false success
# signal (schema.yaml's own MoveTo.status port); `reason`/
# `collision_obstacle` are diagnostic extras alongside it, for whoever
# wants to know WHY a walk that ended early did (a BT leaf's own
# halt-reason log line, a mission report, etc.) without having to
# separately re-poll SafetyMonitor's own state after the fact.

# Chained cubic Bezier control points (length 3k+1), the SAME format
# PlanPath.srv's own control_points response carries.
geometry_msgs/Point[] control_points
---
# Result

# true iff FollowPath ran to natural completion -- false for anything
# that ended the walk early (a safety cutoff, a cancel, an internal
# error). Mirrors MoveTo's own status port in schema.yaml exactly (says
# nothing about final position accuracy; pair with EvaluateCondition's
# DistanceBelow after this action completes, same as problog_project's
# own tree does with its DistanceBelow condition node).
bool status

# One of: "completed" (status true), "battery_depleted",
# "collision_detected" (see collision_obstacle below for which one), or
# "interrupted" -- every other way a walk can end early: an external
# cancel (e.g. the BT itself halting this leaf), max_walk_duration_s
# elapsing with no FollowPath result (see move_to_node.py's own comment
# on why that walk has no Nav2 recovery layer of its own),
# controller_server never accepting the goal, Nav2's own FollowPath
# action reporting failure/abort on its own, or this node failing to
# even start the walk (bad control_points, FollowPath action server
# unavailable, no odom pose, diff_drive_controller never active).
string reason

# The obstacle's own Gazebo collision name (e.g.
# "tree_05::tree_05_link::tree_05_collision") this robot actually hit --
# see safety_monitor.py's own collision_obstacle() -- set ONLY when
# reason is "collision_detected", empty otherwise.
string collision_obstacle
---
# Feedback

# Straight-line distance from the robot's current position to the
# path's own final control point, metres -- forwarded from Nav2's own
# FollowPath feedback of the same name.
float64 distance_to_goal
